1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,891
Important tasking gets buried in email.

2
00:00:02,891 --> 00:00:06,102
PacketRat reads the mail,
finds what needs you,

3
00:00:06,102 --> 00:00:10,748
shows the reason and the source for
every action, and sends nothing.

4
00:00:11,548 --> 00:00:16,377
PacketRat is for anyone whose
important tasking gets buried in email.

5
00:00:16,377 --> 00:00:21,287
It turns an inbox into a short action
queue, with the reason beside every item.

6
00:00:21,987 --> 00:00:25,391
Mail comes in three ways:
exported message files,

7
00:00:25,391 --> 00:00:27,892
classic Outlook, or Microsoft Graph.

8
00:00:27,892 --> 00:00:32,576
This demo reads files. Outlook and
Graph are built, but not verified live.

9
00:00:33,276 --> 00:00:34,805
Everything runs on this PC.

10
00:00:34,805 --> 00:00:39,967
A launcher starts a PowerShell service
on loopback, a browser shows the queue,

11
00:00:39,967 --> 00:00:44,334
and your rules, dismissals and
counts live in a local data folder.

12
00:00:45,034 --> 00:00:48,545
For judgment it calls a model,
always through Fairlead.

13
00:00:48,545 --> 00:00:50,708
PacketRat holds only its own Fairlead key,

14
00:00:50,708 --> 00:00:53,076
and Fairlead gets OpenAI's
key from Lazarette.

15
00:00:53,776 --> 00:00:57,075
The model suggests.
Your rules decide the priority,

16
00:00:57,075 --> 00:01:00,439
and your own overrides and
dismissals win over both.

17
00:01:00,753 --> 00:01:03,674
Out of it come drafts
for review in Outlook,

18
00:01:03,674 --> 00:01:07,657
a counts file for Semaphore,
and a backlog item for Halyard.

19
00:01:07,657 --> 00:01:11,758
PacketRat saves them;
the receiving side is not tried live.

20
00:01:12,458 --> 00:01:15,090
And the rule that matters most:

21
00:01:15,090 --> 00:01:18,317
PacketRat never sends or deletes mail.

22
00:01:18,317 --> 00:01:20,157
A person approves every reply.

23
00:01:20,957 --> 00:01:23,628
Fairlead holds the real model key.

24
00:01:23,628 --> 00:01:27,376
OpenAI's key sits in Lazarette,
the vault on this PC,

25
00:01:27,376 --> 00:01:29,922
and is shared with Fairlead by name.

26
00:01:29,922 --> 00:01:33,057
How it gets there is in the Halyard video.

27
00:01:33,757 --> 00:01:36,194
Every tool in the fleet
gets its own Fairlead key.

28
00:01:36,194 --> 00:01:38,296
Pick PacketRat, install it,

29
00:01:38,296 --> 00:01:41,644
and it lands in Windows
Credential Manager.

30
00:01:41,644 --> 00:01:43,399
PacketRat holds no model key itself.

31
00:01:44,099 --> 00:01:47,391
Back in PacketRat,
Configuration has a Fairlead check.

32
00:01:47,391 --> 00:01:50,916
It is local only: it looks for the key in

33
00:01:50,916 --> 00:01:54,355
Credential Manager and contacts nothing.

34
00:01:54,355 --> 00:01:58,310
Found. PacketRat starts from the launcher.

35
00:01:58,310 --> 00:02:01,379
Two settings are optional:
batch classification,

36
00:02:01,379 --> 00:02:05,343
which gets a typed record for
every message, and your address,

37
00:02:05,343 --> 00:02:08,092
so it knows which mail
is addressed to you.

38
00:02:08,792 --> 00:02:11,583
For mail it can read classic Outlook,

39
00:02:11,583 --> 00:02:14,827
Microsoft Graph,
or exported message files.

40
00:02:14,827 --> 00:02:17,289
Here it reads fourteen invented messages

41
00:02:17,289 --> 00:02:19,875
from a folder, so no mailbox is on screen.

42
00:02:20,575 --> 00:02:22,324
Point it at the folder and save.

43
00:02:23,024 --> 00:02:25,191
Nothing is read until you analyze.

44
00:02:25,991 --> 00:02:29,028
Analyze inbox. PacketRat
reads the folder and

45
00:02:29,028 --> 00:02:32,335
sends the messages to the
model through Fairlead.

46
00:02:32,335 --> 00:02:34,232
A real model, a few seconds.

47
00:02:34,932 --> 00:02:37,480
Fourteen messages classified:

48
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:40,732
eight need a reply, six need nothing.

49
00:02:40,732 --> 00:02:43,333
Every claim links back
to its source message.

50
00:02:44,033 --> 00:02:48,914
And Fairlead's activity log
shows the call: PacketRat's key,

51
00:02:48,914 --> 00:02:53,389
the smart route, served by
OpenAI's GPT four point one.

52
00:02:54,616 --> 00:02:56,964
Overview shows the shape
of the run: open actions,

53
00:02:56,964 --> 00:02:59,172
how many are high priority,
and the next moves.

54
00:02:59,968 --> 00:03:03,360
The action queue lists every
follow-up with its reason.

55
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:06,448
The badge access and the
same-day invoice are High:

56
00:03:06,448 --> 00:03:08,931
the model said so, and the rules kept it.

57
00:03:08,931 --> 00:03:10,412
The rest are Medium.

58
00:03:11,112 --> 00:03:12,788
A person overrides any priority.

59
00:03:12,788 --> 00:03:15,972
Lunch Friday is not tasking:
it goes to Low,

60
00:03:15,972 --> 00:03:19,734
then Dismiss with a reason,
and PacketRat remembers.

61
00:03:20,434 --> 00:03:23,354
Dismissal memory keeps the reason,
and Restore brings the item back.

62
00:03:24,054 --> 00:03:26,205
Overrides and dismissals
survive the next scan.

63
00:03:27,005 --> 00:03:28,972
Draft opens a reply for any action.

64
00:03:28,972 --> 00:03:32,716
Add a line of guidance,
for example: approve it,

65
00:03:32,716 --> 00:03:37,161
and confirm in the finance
portal before fifteen hundred.

66
00:03:37,861 --> 00:03:40,010
Generate draft sends
the item to the model.

67
00:03:40,010 --> 00:03:42,669
One suggested reply, editable,

68
00:03:42,669 --> 00:03:45,239
with no signature of its own.

69
00:03:45,939 --> 00:03:48,774
Copy it. With Outlook
mail, Open in Outlook

70
00:03:48,774 --> 00:03:51,411
puts it above your signature for review.

71
00:03:51,411 --> 00:03:53,627
Nothing is ever sent for you.

72
00:03:54,779 --> 00:03:56,885
The briefing lists the must-reply items.

73
00:03:56,885 --> 00:04:00,774
Each Email number is a verified
source: the reference is

74
00:04:00,774 --> 00:04:04,594
checked against the scan,
so a model cannot invent one.

75
00:04:05,294 --> 00:04:09,526
Pick a section,
pick Action plan, Generate.

76
00:04:09,526 --> 00:04:11,910
The model reads only that section,

77
00:04:11,910 --> 00:04:14,856
and the plan is advice, not a task record.

78
00:04:15,556 --> 00:04:20,617
For the whole report, a leadership
briefing: a status summary to review,

79
00:04:20,617 --> 00:04:24,764
marked as AI interpretation,
never an authoritative record.

80
00:04:25,464 --> 00:04:28,536
And every retained
action keeps its source,

81
00:04:28,536 --> 00:04:32,537
with the sender and the reason,
ready to draft or track.

82
00:04:33,837 --> 00:04:35,171
Priority is explainable.

83
00:04:35,171 --> 00:04:38,318
Configuration lists the rules in order:

84
00:04:38,318 --> 00:04:42,917
security and mandatory action
first, then direct requests

85
00:04:42,917 --> 00:04:46,789
from your organization,
then any direct request,

86
00:04:46,789 --> 00:04:50,743
then replied and newsletters,
which are left out.

87
00:04:50,743 --> 00:04:54,426
First match wins,
and you can add your own.

88
00:04:55,006 --> 00:04:58,656
Dismissal memory keeps what you dismissed,

89
00:04:58,656 --> 00:05:03,610
and what the rules leave out,
each with a Restore button.

90
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:06,054
Every button has a named command.

91
00:05:06,054 --> 00:05:09,120
A script or an agent can show the queue,

92
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:11,267
or analyze, with no browser.

93
00:05:11,977 --> 00:05:14,833
Stats show counts what PacketRat did,

94
00:05:14,833 --> 00:05:17,690
times the minutes assumed per action.

95
00:05:17,690 --> 00:05:21,065
It is an estimate, not a measurement,

96
00:05:21,065 --> 00:05:23,528
and not a score for anyone.

97
00:05:24,228 --> 00:05:28,620
Stats export saves the raw counts
as JSON, and a small script

98
00:05:28,620 --> 00:05:33,157
turns them into a CSV that
Semaphore or a spreadsheet can load.

99
00:05:33,857 --> 00:05:36,856
And item handoff saves a
tracked mail as a backlog

100
00:05:36,856 --> 00:05:39,676
candidate in the format
Halyard's Intake reads.

101
00:05:39,676 --> 00:05:43,068
Title, action, urgency.

102
00:05:43,068 --> 00:05:46,143
No sender, no message body.

103
00:05:47,443 --> 00:05:50,130
The same fourteen messages. By hand,

104
00:05:50,130 --> 00:05:52,667
one person took one minute twenty.

105
00:05:52,667 --> 00:05:56,499
With PacketRat, four seconds to analyze,
then fifty seconds to review its queue.

106
00:05:57,199 --> 00:05:59,453
A modest saving on only fourteen messages.

107
00:05:59,453 --> 00:06:03,748
One person, one run each, not a study.

108
00:06:03,748 --> 00:06:06,587
The burden record keeps both numbers,

109
00:06:06,587 --> 00:06:08,966
and anyone can enter their own.

110
00:06:09,516 --> 00:06:12,952
Verified live in this
recording: exported files,

111
00:06:12,952 --> 00:06:16,460
a real model through Fairlead,
the queue, drafts,

112
00:06:16,460 --> 00:06:21,113
the briefing with checked sources,
and the files PacketRat saves.

113
00:06:21,813 --> 00:06:26,360
Not verified live: classic
Outlook, Microsoft Graph,

114
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:31,695
Semaphore reading the counts,
Halyard importing the hand-off.

115
00:06:31,695 --> 00:06:33,396
Minutes saved is an estimate.

116
00:06:33,396 --> 00:06:35,915
Every message here is invented.

117
00:06:36,615 --> 00:06:39,748
The authority statement is one command.

118
00:06:39,748 --> 00:06:43,518
PacketRat reads mail and proposes work,

119
00:06:43,518 --> 00:06:45,548
and a person decides.

120
00:06:45,548 --> 00:06:49,484
It changes mail or saved
data only on a named command,

121
00:06:49,484 --> 00:06:52,035
and it never sends or deletes mail.

122
00:06:52,735 --> 00:06:56,272
End session stops the local service,
and so does closing the tab.

123
00:06:57,072 --> 00:06:58,914
PacketRat sorts.

124
00:06:58,914 --> 00:07:00,526
People decide.

125
00:07:00,526 --> 00:07:02,252
That's the run.

126
00:07:02,252 --> 00:07:03,519
Fair winds.
